    </Nav>

    <div class="container docs">
      <aside class="toc">
        <p class="toc-title">On this page</p>
        <ol>
          <li><a href="#summary">Summary</a></li>
          <li><a href="#table">Side by side</a></li>
          <li><a href="#easyhost-fits">When EasyHost fits</a></li>
          <li><a href="#vercel-fits">When Vercel fits</a></li>
          <li><a href="#migrate">Moving from Vercel</a></li>
          <li><a href="#faq">FAQ</a></li>
        </ol>
      </aside>

      <main class="doc">
        <div class="doc-head">
          <ol class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
            <li><a href="/">EasyHost</a></li><li aria-current="page">Vercel alternative</li>
          </ol>
          <h1>A free Vercel alternative for Next.js apps</h1>
          <p class="lead">EasyHost is a free place to run a Next.js app when you want a plain always-on server with a database and file storage already attached. Vercel is the better choice when you need a global CDN, preview deployments or Git-based workflows. This page lays out the differences so you can pick.</p>
          <p class="doc-meta">Last updated November 9, 2026</p>
        </div>

        <section id="summary">
          <h2>The summary</h2>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Runtime.</strong> Vercel splits your app into serverless functions behind a CDN. EasyHost runs <code>next start</code> in one container that stays up.</li>
            <li><strong>Backend.</strong> EasyHost includes Postgres and S3 compatible storage in the free account, with transactional email coming soon. On Vercel Hobby, a database and email come from other providers.</li>
            <li><strong>Workflow.</strong> Vercel deploys from Git and gives every branch a preview URL. EasyHost deploys from a CLI that coding agents can drive end to end.</li>
            <li><strong>Reach.</strong> Vercel serves from a global edge network. EasyHost serves from one region.</li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <section id="table">
          <h2>EasyHost and Vercel Hobby side by side</h2>
          <p>Vercel details are from the <a href="https://vercel.com/docs/plans/hobby" rel="noopener">Vercel Hobby plan docs</a> as of September 2026. Those docs also state that the Hobby plan is restricted to non-commercial, personal use.</p>
          <div class="table-wrap">
            <table>
              <thead><tr><th></th><th>EasyHost</th><th>Vercel Hobby</th></tr></thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr><td>Price</td><td>$0, no credit card</td><td>$0</td></tr>
                <tr><td>How the app runs</td><td>One always-on container, 512 MB RAM, 1 CPU</td><td>Serverless functions, up to 300 seconds each</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Monthly usage</td><td>Not metered</td><td>100 GB data transfer, 1,000,000 function invocations, 4 CPU-hours</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Over the limit</td><td>Fixed resources, nothing to exceed</td><td>The feature is paused until 30 days have passed</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Projects</td><td>3</td><td>200</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Postgres</td><td>Included, 1 per project</td><td>Through integrations</td></tr>
                <tr><td>File storage</td><td>S3 compatible bucket, 1 per project</td><td>Vercel Blob</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Email sending</td><td>Coming soon</td><td>Not included</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Git deploys and previews</td><td>No</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Global CDN</td><td>No</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Frameworks</td><td>Node.js apps, including Next.js</td><td>Many</td></tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section id="easyhost-fits">
          <h2>When EasyHost is the better fit</h2>
          <h3>You want a server, not functions</h3>
          <p>Your app is a single Node.js process that stays running. In-memory caches survive between requests, there are no cold starts, and you do not need to think about function size or invocation counts.</p>
          <h3>You want the backend in the same place</h3>
          <p>A side project usually needs a database and somewhere to put uploads. On EasyHost those are two commands in the same CLI: <code>ezhst db create</code> and <code>ezhst storage create</code>. Email is coming soon. See <a href="/free-postgres-hosting">free Postgres hosting</a> for the database details.</p>
          <h3>Your coding agent does the deploying</h3>
          <p>Every <code>ezhst</code> command takes <code>--json</code>, never prompts without a terminal, and returns errors an agent can act on. Sign-up works from the command line too: after you give the agent the 6-digit code from your inbox, it can go from an empty account to a live URL without a browser. The <a href="/guides/deploy-nextjs-with-ai-agents">agent deploy guide</a> shows how.</p>
          <h3>You want limits you can predict</h3>
          <p>There is no usage meter. An app gets 512 MB of RAM and 1 CPU, and that is what it has at any traffic level.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="vercel-fits">
          <h2>When Vercel is the better fit</h2>
          <p>EasyHost is young and deliberately small. Choose Vercel if any of these matter to you:</p>
          <ul>
            <li>Visitors all over the world and you need pages served from an edge location near them.</li>
            <li>Preview deployments for every pull request, or deploys triggered by a Git push.</li>
            <li>Traffic beyond what one 512 MB container can serve.</li>
            <li>Team features, analytics, firewall rules and the rest of a mature platform.</li>
            <li>Applications that cannot run as a Node.js service.</li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <section id="migrate">
          <h2>Moving a Next.js app from Vercel to EasyHost</h2>
          <ol>
            <li>Pull your environment variables from Vercel into a local file, then import them.</li>
            <li>Create a project, link the folder and deploy.</li>
            <li>Add your domain and point DNS at EasyHost.</li>
          </ol>
          <div class="code"><pre>vercel env pull .env.production
npx ezhst projects create my-app --link
npx ezhst env import .env.production
npx ezhst deploy
npx ezhst domains add www.example.com</pre></div>
          <h3>Things to check first</h3>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Vercel-only packages.</strong> Replace <code>@vercel/blob</code> with any S3 client pointed at your EasyHost bucket, and point your Postgres client at the EasyHost connection string.</li>
            <li><strong>Cron jobs in <code>vercel.json</code>.</strong> EasyHost does not read <code>vercel.json</code>, so scheduled routes will not be called.</li>
            <li><strong>Edge runtime.</strong> Everything runs in the one Node.js container in a single region.</li>
            <li><strong>Image optimization.</strong> <code>next/image</code> is handled by the Next.js server inside your container.</li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <section id="faq">
          <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
          <h3>Is EasyHost a drop-in replacement for Vercel?</h3>
          <p>For a standard Next.js app, mostly yes: it builds with <code>next build</code> and runs with <code>next start</code> without changes. Vercel-specific services such as Vercel Blob, <code>vercel.json</code> cron jobs, preview deployments and the edge network do not carry over.</p>
          <h3>Is EasyHost faster than Vercel?</h3>
          <p>It depends on where your visitors are. EasyHost has no cold starts because the container is always on, but it serves from one region. Vercel serves static assets from a global CDN.</p>
          <h3>Can I keep my custom domain when I move?</h3>
          <p>Yes. Add it with <code>ezhst domains add</code>, set the DNS record that <code>ezhst domains list</code> shows, then run <code>ezhst domains verify</code> to issue HTTPS. Details in the <a href="/docs#domains">domains docs</a>.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="start">
          <h2>Start for free</h2>
          <p>Install the skill, choose Global or Local, then tell your agent to deploy. No credit card, no config files.</p>
          <div class="code"><pre>npx ezhst skill install</pre></div>
          <div class="doc-cta">
            <a href="https://app.easyhost.fun/register" class="btn btn-primary">Sign up free</a>
            <a href="/docs" class="btn btn-outline">Read the docs</a>
          </div>
        </section>
      </main>
    </div>
    <Footer />
    <CopyCode />
</Base>
